            <ArticleTitle>Ultrasound in sciatica.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>393-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Investigation of the sagittal diameter of the spinal canal by ultrasound has shown a relative stenosis in patients with sciatica. In this study 12 patients who had recovered uneventfully after being operated on for herniated lumbar discs were found to have a broader spinal canal than 10 patients treated in the same way but still suffering from pain. Moreover, more difficulty was experienced investigating this latter group with the described &quot;B-scan&quot; technique. It is suggested that a preoperative ultrasound investigation would be able to indicate a narrow spinal canal and thereby reduce the number of disc operation failures.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
